  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 143 PM CDT Thu Oct 22 2026\n\nA mid/upper ridge continues to build over the central Plains \nwhile a vigorous trough is digging into the southwestern CONUS, \ncentered near southern California. At the surface, a broad lee \ntrough extends from the High Plains into eastern Colorado, \nmaintaining a southerly low- level jet and warm advection into \nKansas. This pattern supports continued seasonably warm \ntemperatures and mostly dry conditions through Saturday.\n\nAcross the area, skies will remain mostly clear to partly \ncloudy today, with increasing mid and high clouds tonight into \nFriday as moisture and upper-level cloud shields spread eastward\nahead of the western trough. Afternoon highs today and Friday \nare expected to reach the mid to upper 70s, with some locations \nin central and western Kansas climbing into the low 80s, well \nbelow record values but several degrees above late October \nnormals.\n\nAs the lee trough deepens Friday, the surface pressure gradient\nwill tighten, especially over central and western Kansas. This \nwill yield sustained southerly winds in the 15 to 25 mph range \nwith gusts into the mid-20s to perhaps near 30 mph west of the \nFlint Hills, while southeast counties \n(Chanute\u2013Parsons\u2013Coffeyville corridor) see somewhat lighter but \nstill noticeable south winds. These breezy conditions will \npersist into Saturday, supporting good mixing and limiting \novernight cooling, with lows mainly in the mid to upper 50s.\n\nThe more consequential period begins late Sunday into Monday as\nthe stubborn southwestern CONUS trough finally ejects east- \nnortheast toward the central Plains. As height falls spread into\nKansas, large-scale ascent, deepening moisture, and \nstrengthening low-level convergence along and ahead of the \nattendant surface front will support increasing shower and \nembedded thunderstorm coverage.\n\nEnsembles favor higher precipitation chances over central and \nwestern portions of the area (e.g., Hutchinson, Great Bend, \nSalina corridors) with lower but non-zero chances extending into\nthe Wichita metro and southeast Kansas. Timing remains somewhat\nuncertain, but trends support showers developing late Sunday \nafternoon or evening in western zones, spreading east Sunday \nnight and persisting at least into Monday. \n\nRainfall amounts are generally favored to be light as the \npattern suggests scattered to numerous showers with locally \nmoderate rainfall rates rather than a widespread heavy rain \nevent. The greatest risk for more appreciable totals would be \nover western and north central Kansas where forcing and moisture\noverlap longest. \n\nBehind the front and main wave, cooler air filters into Kansas \nMonday and Tuesday.",
